1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,006
Lesson 7.3.

2
00:00:02,386 --> 00:00:04,766
Random walks on Cayley graphs.

3
00:00:05,883 --> 00:00:08,872
Imagine placing a drop
of heat on one node of a

4
00:00:08,872 --> 00:00:11,734
graph and letting it
spread to its neighbors.

5
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:15,640
Over time, the heat becomes
uniform, and its final value

6
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,851
at each node is one divided
by the number of nodes.

7
00:00:19,231 --> 00:00:22,004
If the graph is a group whose
size is the order we are

8
00:00:22,004 --> 00:00:24,981
looking for, measuring that
final value reveals the order.

9
00:00:25,361 --> 00:00:28,357
Today we build that graph
and understand its dynamics.

10
00:00:29,483 --> 00:00:34,721
Take b = 3 modulo two hundred
ninety-nine, whose order is thirty-three.

11
00:00:35,101 --> 00:00:39,263
The nodes are the powers of b: a
cycle of thirty-three elements.

12
00:00:39,643 --> 00:00:45,379
The edges multiply by b^(±
2^t), with t from zero to M = 9.

13
00:00:45,759 --> 00:00:52,669
In exponent coordinates, each node j
connects to j ± 2^t modulo thirty-three.

14
00:00:53,049 --> 00:00:55,480
The key point: the
neighbors are computed with

15
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:58,121
modular multiplication,
without knowing the order.

16
00:00:59,250 --> 00:01:03,155
The walker, with probability one
half, stays put; with the other

17
00:01:03,155 --> 00:01:06,511
half, it jumps along one of
the twenty edges at random.

18
00:01:06,891 --> 00:01:10,631
That is the matrix W = ½(I + P).

19
00:01:11,011 --> 00:01:16,472
Starting with all the mass on the
identity, the distribution after

20
00:01:16,472 --> 00:01:21,106
ℓ steps is p_ℓ = W^ℓ δ_e: the
discrete-time heat kernel.

21
00:01:21,486 --> 00:01:24,761
Its difference between steps
is the Laplacian applied

22
00:01:24,761 --> 00:01:27,603
to the distribution: a
discrete heat equation.

23
00:01:28,717 --> 00:01:34,058
Since the graph is a cyclic
group, the functions χ_k(j) =

24
00:01:34,058 --> 00:01:40,336
e^(2πikj/r), the characters we saw
in lesson 0.6, are eigenvectors.

25
00:01:40,716 --> 00:01:46,086
The eigenvalue of P is the
average of the cosines of

26
00:01:46,086 --> 00:01:50,733
2πk2^t/r, and that of
W is λ_k = (1 + μ_k)/2.

27
00:01:51,113 --> 00:01:55,660
They all lie between zero and one, and
only the zero mode equals exactly one.

28
00:01:56,783 --> 00:02:01,500
Expanding the delta in
characters, the value

29
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,002
at the identity is p_ℓ(e) = (1/r) Σ λ_k^ℓ.

30
00:02:06,382 --> 00:02:11,687
The k = 0 term gives exactly
one over r; the others decay.

31
00:02:12,067 --> 00:02:18,553
In the example, after five steps it
is 0.079; after ten, 0.036; after

32
00:02:18,553 --> 00:02:24,193
twenty, 0.0304, already very
close to one over thirty-three.

33
00:02:24,573 --> 00:02:28,000
Computed with the spectral
formula or by multiplying the

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,875
matrix, the result agrees
to machine precision.

35
00:02:31,983 --> 00:02:33,380
Why powers of two?

36
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:37,925
With only ± 1 jumps, the graph
is a cycle and the slowest

37
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:41,797
eigenvalue is 0.99: seven
hundred sixty-two steps are

38
00:02:41,797 --> 00:02:45,450
needed for the modes to
drop below one thousandth.

39
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:51,751
With the dyadic generators, the slowest
is 0.669, and eighteen steps suffice.

40
00:02:52,131 --> 00:02:55,362
Powers of two scatter any
frequency: that is the

41
00:02:55,362 --> 00:02:58,457
idea of the doubling
lemma in the next lesson.

42
00:02:59,583 --> 00:03:03,399
Heat kernels on groups
describe mixing times of Markov

43
00:03:03,399 --> 00:03:07,426
chains, such as shuffling
cards; they appear in diffusion

44
00:03:07,426 --> 00:03:11,312
maps and graph neural networks,
and in expander graphs,

45
00:03:11,692 --> 00:03:14,035
which mix fast with few generators.

46
00:03:14,415 --> 00:03:17,289
In lab ten you will build this walk.

47
00:03:18,417 --> 00:03:21,780
Common mistakes: forgetting
laziness, without which there may

48
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:24,702
be an eigenvalue of minus
one that prevents settling.

49
00:03:25,082 --> 00:03:27,813
Believing the order is
needed to build the graph.

50
00:03:28,193 --> 00:03:31,206
Confusing the eigenvalues of P and of W.

51
00:03:31,586 --> 00:03:34,414
And simulating the full
vector digitally, which

52
00:03:34,414 --> 00:03:36,881
costs r per step and loses the advantage.

53
00:03:37,983 --> 00:03:41,710
In summary: the Cayley
graph of ⟨b⟩ with dyadic

54
00:03:41,710 --> 00:03:45,357
generators is built
without knowing the order.

55
00:03:45,737 --> 00:03:51,433
The half-lazy walk is a discrete heat
equation, diagonalized by characters.

56
00:03:51,813 --> 00:03:55,106
Its value at the identity
tends to one over r, and

57
00:03:55,106 --> 00:03:58,070
powers of two make it
get there in few steps.

58
00:03:59,183 --> 00:04:02,031
To practice: write the
neighbors of a node.

59
00:04:02,411 --> 00:04:05,169
Prove that the characters
are eigenvectors.

60
00:04:05,549 --> 00:04:09,586
Explain why the eigenvalues
of W lie between zero and one.

61
00:04:09,966 --> 00:04:13,105
And derive the heat-kernel
formula at the identity.
